nodecode-cospark is a rewrite of corift's corift/cospark-ai:

  https://github.com/corift/cospark-ai
  commit 535535a8006b5b6b44c6a3acefa6273cf9d26a0c
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Cospark (LICENSE)

The plugin, at the folder plugins/cospark, was a portable Agent Plugins v1 package with no code of its own: plugin.json, a Codex manifest, an .mcp.json declaring the hosted MCP server at https://api.cospark.so/mcp (Streamable HTTP, browser OAuth), a marketplace entry, the icon, and one skill, skills/cospark/SKILL.md, that taught a model to connect to that server, read its documentation first, work within the scope and approval it was given, and inspect what it produced. This rewrite speaks that server's MCP wire itself, in Common Lisp over nlk:http -- initialize, the Mcp-Session-Id, tools/list, tools/call, an answer that is either a JSON object or a one-event stream -- so every tool the server offers is reachable by the name the server gives it, behind three tools: cospark_docs (the server's read_docs, so the maintained guides are the first call), cospark_tools (what the server offers now) and cospark_call (one of them by name). The skill, whole, is the manual topic (help :cospark) and its one-line summary in every request, and the rule it stated in prose -- nothing that spends without the person's clear yes -- is enforced by cospark_call, which refuses a tool the server does not mark read-only until confirmed is true. What was left out: the manifests, which the cell contract replaces with a section and a declaration; the OAuth browser flow, which the shipped mcp cell already owns, so this cell reuses that sign-in, a pasted token, an environment variable or a file; and a purpose-named tool for each of the server's tools, because the repository carries no schema for them and a list restated here would go stale. Nothing of the original is vendored: there was no code to vendor.
